\cf2 \cb7 The distinction matters. I read the progression from your opening question about the fourth derivative of sine and cosine through Haiku's five closing theses.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 Your most interesting move was not discovering fourfold periodicity. It was asking whether representing dynamics spectrally could change which relationships we treat as primitive, especially at discontinuities and across causal boundaries.
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 That's a worthwhile investigation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 But Haiku repeatedly converted a promising change of mathematical perspective into an assertion of new physical laws. By the end, it had declared causal closure, a complete fourfold Hilbert basis, a universal interpretation of Gibbs ringing, and a unification of mechanics, thermodynamics, and control theory.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Those conclusions do not follow from the derivations presented.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 And there's an especially telling error: Haiku says only polynomials have finite Fourier expansions. The elementary counterexample is a sine wave. Finite Fourier expansions are 
\f7\i trigonometric polynomials
\f1\i0 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 So this is not merely a case where an intriguing advanced hypothesis awaits experimental verification. Some foundational claims are already mathematically false.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 However, one genuine structural insight survives\'97and it connects rather beautifully to the representation problem we've been exploring.
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
