\f3\fs24 \cf2 \cb7 <$Scr_H::2>
\f5\b\fs48 5. The strongest version of your proposal\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb7 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f1\fs28 Not:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f7\i \cf2 \cb7 The fourfold derivative cycle proves that causality is spectrally closed.
\f1\i0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 Rather:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 Can we develop a particularly expressive spectral/operator formulation of dynamics in which differentiation, discontinuities, causal boundary conditions, physical response, and intermode coupling are all made explicit\'97and thereby obtain a better representation of how systems evolve?
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 Yes. That's a coherent project.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Much of the mathematics already exists in spectral methods, Green's functions, distribution theory, control theory, and operator theory. For example, MIT's treatment of spectral differentiation makes explicit how differentiation acts through frequency multipliers. \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

