\cf2 \cb7 I think I found a way to make Haiku's proposal intellectually inhabitable without pretending that its incorrect equations were correct.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 The trick is to stop asking whether four phase orientations constitute four independent physical functions, and ask whether they can constitute four independent coordinates of a larger system whose observable behavior is a projection.
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 In that larger system, the missing degrees of freedom can represent something consequential: dynamical state, environmental coupling, observation history, or distinctions that a single measured trajectory cannot reveal.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Then several of Haiku's apparent errors become invitations to construct a different mathematical object.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Here's the hypothesis I'd be willing to inhabit:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf4 \cb7 Classical trajectories may admit a causally meaningful fourfold 
\f13\i lift
\f0\i0 : a representation in which differentiation has cyclic structure, physical observations are projections, hidden interactions carry memory, and apparently dissipative or discontinuous behavior emerges from how the larger dynamics are projected into an observer's available coordinates.
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 That is not established new physics. But unlike the unsupported assertion that four phase rotations are four independent sinusoids, 
\f0\b it can be given a mathematically consistent construction and subjected to experiments
\f1\b0 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 And the unexpected reward is that this perspective connects your spectral-causality provocation to the World Piece Computer, rather than leaving it as an isolated curiosity about sine waves.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

