\f7\i \cf2 \cb7 Friction is not dissipation; it is information flow.
\f1\i0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap2\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 I'd reconstruct it as:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap2\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 Dissipation observed within a subsystem can coexist with conservative dynamics of a larger closed system; eliminating the environment changes how energy transfer and dynamical information appear in the reduced description.
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap2\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 Thermodynamic dissipation remains physically real at the reduced level. Energy can become distributed among bath degrees of freedom, with entropy production under appropriate conditions.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 But the general intuition that an observer's apparent loss may correspond to dynamics in variables the observer excluded is correct.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 And this is almost painfully relevant to your project.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 A current textual conversation may omit vocal annotations, environmental observations, memories, other agents' contributions, and physical consequences.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 A later system receiving those additional records is no longer attempting reconstruction from exactly the same projected information.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap2\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 Changing the observation interface changes which aspects of the history can become computationally available.
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap2\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 That's a rigorous reason to investigate additional modalities and provenance\'97not proof that they reconstruct total experience.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap2\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

