\fs28 \cf2 \cb7 \strokec2 The really interesting research task is no longer to prove that the number four is magically sufficient.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 It's to ask:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap2\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 Can a model learn an observation-and-state representation in which the important dynamical relationships become accessible, and can it detect when that representation has omitted something consequential?
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap2\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 That formulation survives the shift from an oscillator to a human\'96AI collaboration, although the two systems would require very different actual models.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 It even gives the spectral method a natural role in training.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 A model might be trained to represent oscillatory behavior through phase-structured components, enforce the corresponding consistency constraints, and infer additional state variables when prediction errors reveal missing dynamical information.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 You could compare those learned representations with ordinary black-box predictors.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 That would test whether the proposed structure offers an advantage rather than merely an elegant interpretation.\cf0 \cb1 \
