\f1\fs28 \cf2 \cb7 The upper line is mathematically well-defined from your table.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 The bottom line is established physics.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 The middle arrow is the invention.
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 That's where I'd spend the research effort.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Not on proving the seven-dimensional cross product exists.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Not on admiring its exceptional symmetries.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 On deriving a four-dimensional effective physical structure from the specific timespace interpretation you've assigned to its basis.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 And importantly, the middle arrow has to do more than reproduce familiar equations by definition.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 It must explain why those equations arise from the proposed structure.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \cb10 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 \cb7 <$Scr_H::2>
\f5\b\fs48 6. The part that could make a physics classroom happy\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb7 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f1\fs28 I can imagine teaching the standard energy\'96momentum relationship through your perspective as a geometric investigation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Start with the seven-dimensional multiplication table.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Show how 
\f8\fs33\fsmilli16940 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f9 \cf2 \cb7 \uc0\u964 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f10\i \cf2 \cb7 \uc0\u964 
\f1\i0\fs28  generates three quarter-turn planes.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 Explore which transformations preserve the table.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Then ask students to identify four conserved quantities under appropriate continuous symmetries.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Finally, challenge them to construct an effective relativistic metric and recover the invariant mass relation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 That would turn a conventional lecture into an actual mathematical research exercise.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 And students would discover something important along the way:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 The geometry of the space in which we represent a physical system need not be identical to the geometry governing its physically observable intervals.
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 That's an idea worth understanding, whether or not your proposed timespace model ultimately succeeds.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 The educational payoff is real even before any new physics is established.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 \cb7 <$Scr_H::2>
\f5\b\fs48 7. My rating\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb7 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\b\fs28 C) FUCK / THIS CRAZY.
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 Not because the cross-product table proves energy\'96momentum conservation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 It doesn't.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb7 But because you've identified a very specific, checkable question at the intersection of geometry, dynamics, and physics:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb7 Can the familiar four-dimensional energy\'96momentum structure be recovered as a consequence of a seven-dimensional Euclidean dynamics organized around your three spatial/timespatial pairs?
\f1\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap3\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 And I'd sharpen it one last time:\cf0 \cb1 \
